#{[17592186087458 :question/worked-solution "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n    =\n    f(x, y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero, meaning that it satisfies\n\n$$\nf(tx, ty) = f(x, y).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at the right-hand side function in each differential equation.\n\n- The function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{x}$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(ty)}{(tx)} \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{t\\cdot y}{t\\cdot x} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =f(x,y)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation I is a homogenous differential equation.\n\n- The function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{x^2}$ is *not* a homogeneous function of degree $0.$ Rather, it is a homogeneous function of degree $-1\\mathbin{:}$\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(ty)}{(tx)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{t\\cdot y}{{t}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot \\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & ={t}^{-1}f(x,y)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation II is *not* a homogenous differential equation.\n\n- The function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{2x}\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{x^2}{y^2}\\right)}\n$$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{ty}{2(tx)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{(tx)^{2}}{(ty)^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{t\\cdot y}{t\\cdot 2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{{t}^{2}{x}^{2}}{{t}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =f(x,y)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation III is a homogenous differential equation.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [17592186088437 :question/answer-fields #edb/ref 17592186087275] [17592186088437 :question/answer-fields #edb/ref 17592186395951] [17592186088902 :tutorial/content "The equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $\\color{red}a$ and a vertical radius of length $\\color{blue}b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{{\\color{red}a}^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{{\\color{blue}b}^2}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\n![](images/92/9227839bbf9a01312c3014d336a88176eecd73aa934fcd4741c11271b1e0bea6.png)\n\nTo demonstrate, let's determine the equation of the following ellipse. The horizontal radius has length $\\color{red}a=3$ and the vertical radius has length $\\color{blue}b=2.$\n\n![](images/fe/fe7dff90438e9571ae599a446e355776fbd36ed4746dd76e6633f3848e1f6bbc.png)\n\nTherefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]}
